%description:

Tcp.timestampSupport:on -- the SYN offers timestamps.

The claim (Tcp.ned): "Timestamps (RFC 7323) support (header option)".

The classes: on (true, the default), off (false; LegacyTcp), changed (a change during the run,
because the parameter is @mutable: each connection reads it at its open). This test separates "on" from "off".

Host under test: host1. Observation: level 1, the SYN of host1 has a Timestamps option.

%# parameter: Tcp.timestampSupport
%# class: on
%# value: true
%# key: *.host1.tcp.timestampSupport
%# expected-result: PASS

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "TcpMutations.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("TcpTimestampSupportOn")
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == true && tcp.ackBit == false")
                  .first()
                  .assertEvent([](const MatchContext& context) { return tcpOptionCount(context.event.packet, inet::tcp::TCPOPTION_TIMESTAMP) == 1; })
                  .describe("the SYN of host1, with a Timestamps option")
                  .within(0.5));
}

%activity:
EV << "TcpTimestampSupportOn compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host1.tcp.timestampSupport = true    # the parameter under test
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST TcpTimestampSupportOn: PASS
